*This is My Ledger, the personal side of v64otd.com. This series is history first: what happened, with sources. Where I give my own opinion, it's marked.*

> *Disclosure: I endorsed independent write-in candidate Jenn Mack for Texas governor and voted Libertarian in most of my other races this fall.*

> **Ballot access:** the rules a candidate or party must meet to have their name printed on the ballot. In most states, the Democratic and Republican nominees qualify automatically. Everyone else has to earn it.

### From Machine to Lockdown

Here's the story so far. The Founders warned against parties and built them anyway (Part 2). The machine era turned them into patronage empires (Part 3). The Progressives broke the bosses but wrote the parties into state law (Part 4). And for most of American history, the parties and the legislatures they ran decided who could vote at all (Part 5).

Today, nearly every adult citizen can vote. So the fight moved. The question isn't who can vote anymore. It's whom you're allowed to vote for. That's the lockdown, and both parties built it.

### Lock 1: The Ballot

When states started printing official ballots, they had to decide who went on them, and the people writing those rules were Democrats and Republicans.

Take Texas. An independent candidate for governor in 2026 needed 81,030 valid signatures, equal to 1% of the total vote in the last governor's race. The signatures had to be gathered after the primaries and turned in by June 25. And here's the catch: anyone who voted in the Democratic or Republican primary for that office wasn't allowed to sign. In practice, the most engaged voters in the state were off-limits.

Minor parties face a separate test. To keep its place on the Texas ballot without petitioning again, a party must meet a 2% vote threshold in statewide races. The major parties don't have to worry about any of that. Their nominees appear automatically.

My own candidate for governor this year, Jenn Mack, ran as a write-in. Rules like these make getting onto the printed ballot a full-time job before a campaign even starts.

The Supreme Court has drawn some lines. In 1968, it ordered Ohio to put independent George Wallace on the presidential ballot, ruling that a state couldn't use its rules to shield the two established parties from competition. But in case after case since then, the Court has upheld many requirements that burden typical third parties and independents, and struck down only the most extreme ones.

### Lock 2: The Sore Loser

Lose your party's primary? In 48 states, you're barred from running in November as an independent or as another party's candidate. These "sore loser" laws began in Mississippi in 1906, and most states adopted them between 1970 and 1995. Iowa added one as recently as 2021. Only Connecticut and New York have none.

**The Texas version.** Texas has had a sore loser law since at least 1977, and its current form is Section 162.015 of the Texas Election Code. It goes further than most people realize. It doesn't only stop primary *candidates*. For state and county offices, anyone who simply *voted* in a party's primary can't run in that November's general election as an independent for an office that was on that primary ballot, or as another party's nominee. And a candidate who lost a primary can't even sign up as a write-in candidate for the same office.

**Two senators the law would have erased.** You don't have to imagine what these laws take away from voters. Two U.S. senators, one from each party, show it.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; **Joe Lieberman, Democrat, Connecticut, 2006.** Lieberman lost his party's primary to Ned Lamont, 52% to 48%. Connecticut has no sore loser law, so he ran in November on his own ballot line, "Connecticut for Lieberman," and won with almost 50% of the vote. Lamont, the party's nominee, got about 40%.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; **Lisa Murkowski, Republican, Alaska, 2010.** Murkowski lost her primary to Joe Miller, who was backed by the Tea Party. She ran as a write-in and won, 100,868 votes to 90,448, the first person elected to the Senate by write-in since Strom Thurmond in 1954.

In both cases, a small primary electorate said no, and the full electorate in November said yes. Under rules like Texas's, neither of them would have been allowed to ask.

To be fair, both were sitting senators with money and name recognition. That's exactly the point: if the law can stop an incumbent, think about what it does to a newcomer with a following and no party behind them.

**The defense.** The Supreme Court upheld this kind of law in *Storer v. Brown* in 1974, 6–3, in a case about California's version of it. The Court said the state's interest in political stability was compelling, and that primary losers shouldn't be able to use the general election to keep fighting a battle they'd already lost. Supporters today make the same case: the laws protect the integrity of the primary, prevent voter confusion, and keep a party from splintering. Justice William Brennan, dissenting, accepted that those goals were legitimate but asked whether they "cannot be served equally well in significantly less burdensome ways."

Here's the part that should stop every reader of this series cold. To justify the law, the Court wrote that California "believes with the Founding Fathers that splintered parties and unrestrained factionalism may do significant damage to the fabric of government," and it cited Madison's *Federalist No. 10*.

> *Editorial:* Read that again. Madison wrote *Federalist No. 10* to warn against faction, as Part 2 showed. The Court used his words to protect the two biggest factions in the country from competition. That's the whole Faction Files in one sentence.

Sore loser laws don't protect voters. They protect parties. A primary is a party choosing its nominee, and it's usually decided by a small, highly partisan slice of the electorate; as Lock 3 shows, about 7% of eligible voters decided most House seats in 2024. That's the party's business. Who the rest of us may vote for in November is ours. As Eli Lehrer of the R Street Institute, a free-market think tank, put it, these laws "entrench party insiders and empower extreme activist groups."

Remember Part 5, too. For two decades, the Texas Democratic Party argued that it was a private club that could decide who could take part in its primary. Sore loser laws flip that argument around: the party gets to act like a private club when it picks its nominee, then uses state law to decide who everyone else is allowed to see on the November ballot. If a candidate loses a primary, the party can refuse to nominate them. It shouldn't be able to stop the voters from hiring them anyway.

### Lock 3: The Primary That Decides Everything

Most House seats are so safely drawn for one party that the only election that matters is that party's primary. In 2024, according to Unite America, a nonpartisan election reform group, primaries effectively decided 380 of 435 House seats, about 87%, and only about 7% of eligible voters cast ballots in those deciding primaries.

So about 1 in 14 eligible Americans picked nearly 9 in 10 members of the House. In states with closed primaries, independents, now the largest group of voters in the country, can't vote in those deciding elections at all.

Voters have tried to change this, and the parties fought back. In 2024, ballot measures to open primaries, create ranked-choice voting, or both failed in Arizona, Colorado, Idaho, Montana, Nevada, Oregon, and South Dakota. The main opponents in those campaigns were the two major parties. As one reform advocate put it afterward, "The status quo won this year."

### Lock 4: The Map

Then there's gerrymandering, the drawing of district lines to guarantee one party wins. Both parties do it, and in 2025 they turned it into an open arms race.

At President Trump's urging, Texas Republicans redrew the state's congressional map mid-decade, aiming to gain five seats. California Democrats answered with Proposition 50, approved by voters in November 2025, which set aside the maps drawn by the state's independent commission and handed the job to the Democratic-controlled legislature, aiming for up to five Democratic seats. Republicans in Missouri and North Carolina redrew too. Each side said it was only responding to the other.

The result in 2026: when the lines are drawn for one party, the primary becomes the real election, and the cycle in Lock 3 repeats. One casualty this week was Rep. Kevin Kiley of California, the former Republican who left his party, refused to caucus with either side, and proposed committee seats for independents. He lost to a Democrat in a district newly redrawn under Proposition 50.

### Lock 5: The Referees

Even the agencies that referee elections are built for two teams.

The Federal Election Commission, which enforces campaign finance law, has six commissioners. By law, no more than three can belong to the same party, and four votes are needed for any official action. In practice, that has meant three Democrats and three Republicans, a structure called bipartisan, but one that leaves no seat for anyone outside the two parties, and that can deadlock whenever the two sides disagree.

And as we covered in Part 1, the Commission on Presidential Debates was created in 1987 by the chairmen of the two parties. Since 2000, it has required candidates to reach 15% in national polls to get on stage. It's almost impossible to reach 15% without the attention the debates provide.

That's the attention lock, and the news business reinforces it. Coverage is built around a two-team fight. A study by the Media Research Center, a conservative media watchdog, of the ABC, CBS, and NBC evening newscasts from January through August 2016 found Donald Trump got 1,773 minutes and Hillary Clinton 1,020 minutes. Libertarian Gary Johnson, who was on the ballot in all 50 states, got 11 seconds. Green Party candidate Jill Stein got one 3-second mention that didn't include her name. Without coverage, candidates can't rise in the polls; without the polls, they can't get on the debate stage; without the debates, they can't get coverage. Social media hasn't changed the pattern. In my experience, it's made it worse.

### Lock 6: Congress Itself

Finally, as Part 1 explained, committee seats in Congress are handed out through the two-party caucuses. An independent who refuses to join one generally gets none. That's why most independents in Congress caucus with one side, and why the label so often turns out to be a costume.

### Lock 7: The Money

The last lock may be the biggest, and it was installed by the Supreme Court.

On January 21, 2010, in *Citizens United v. FEC*, the Supreme Court ruled 5–4 that the government can't limit independent political spending by corporations and unions. "There is no such thing as too much speech," Justice Anthony Kennedy wrote for the majority. In dissent, Justice John Paul Stevens warned, "A democracy cannot function effectively when its constituent members believe laws are being bought and sold." About two months later, a federal appeals court applied that reasoning in *SpeechNow.org v. FEC* and created the super PAC: a group that can take unlimited contributions as long as it doesn't officially coordinate with a candidate.

The money that followed is staggering. In the 2024 elections, according to OpenSecrets, outside groups spent a record $4.5 billion, more than half of it from groups that don't fully disclose their funding. The 2026 midterms cost close to a record $12 billion. And the biggest super PACs weren't independent of the parties in any meaningful sense:

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Senate Leadership Fund (Republican): $489 million

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; MAGA Inc. (Republican): $353 million

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Senate Majority PAC (Democratic): $248 million

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; House Majority PAC (Democratic): $192 million

Those four groups work hand in glove with their parties' leadership. That's the Uniparty's money machine: each side's donors fund each side's super PAC, and together they set a price of entry no independent or third-party candidate can match.

This lock is big enough to get its own chapter. Part 7, The Megaphone, follows the money from the first TV campaign ads in 1952 to this year's $573 million Texas Senate race, and looks at the press, dark money, AI ads, and the free-speech case against new limits.

### Lock 8: The Electoral College's Winner-Take-All Rule

The Constitution lets each state decide how to award its electoral votes. Almost every state chose the same rule: whoever wins the state's popular vote, even by one vote, gets all of its electors. Only Maine, since 1972, and Nebraska, since 1992, split some of theirs by congressional district.

That rule is why third-party presidential candidates rarely win a single elector, no matter how many votes they get. In 1992, Texas's own Ross Perot won about 18.9% of the national popular vote, nearly 20 million votes, more than any candidate outside the two parties since Theodore Roosevelt in 1912. He took part in all three presidential debates. He won zero electoral votes. He didn't carry a single state, though he finished second in Maine and Utah.

When nearly 1 in 5 voters can choose a candidate and get nothing for it, the message to the next voter is clear: don't waste your vote. That's the "wasted vote" fear from Part 1, built into the rules.

**A third idea: the national popular vote pact.** Since 2006, a group of states has been building a workaround called the National Popular Vote Interstate Compact. Each member state agrees to give all of its electoral votes to whoever wins the national popular vote, even if that candidate lost the state. The pact takes effect only when its members hold 270 electoral votes, enough to decide the presidency. Maryland joined first, in 2007. Today, 18 states and the District of Columbia have joined, with 222 electoral votes among them. Virginia was the latest, in April 2026. Texas is not a member.

The members are almost all Democratic-led states. Republican-led legislative chambers have advanced the pact in several states, but no Republican governor has signed it. Colorado's voters kept their state in it in a 2020 ballot vote.

Supporters argue it uses a power the Constitution already gives the states. Article II lets each legislature decide how to choose its electors, and winner-take-all isn't in the Constitution either. They say it would make every vote count the same and force candidates to campaign everywhere, not just in a handful of swing states, and they point to 2000 and 2016, when the winner of the national popular vote lost the presidency.

Critics call it an "urban power grab" that would pull attention away from rural America and undo the federal balance the Founders built. Legal scholars disagree about whether it needs the consent of Congress, or even a constitutional amendment. The Congressional Research Service has called it an open question likely to end up in court.

> *Editorial:* In my opinion, the pact flies in the face of the Electoral College. The Founders deliberately made the Constitution hard to amend. If most Americans want to elect the president by national popular vote, the honest way to do it is an amendment, approved by two-thirds of Congress and three-quarters of the states. This pact gets the same result without ever winning that support, and it could force a state to hand its electors to a candidate its own voters rejected. It wouldn't help independents or third parties, either. It counts the national plurality, so the "wasted vote" fear stays right where it is. The better fixes keep the Electoral College and change winner-take-all by splitting electors by district, as Maine and Nebraska do, or by allocating them in proportion to each state's vote.

### Lock 9: Who Controls the Floor

Committee seats (Lock 6) are only the start. The bigger power in Congress lies in the agenda: in the House, the Speaker and the majority leadership decide which bills get a vote at all, and the Senate majority leader controls what reaches the Senate floor. A bill can have majority support and still never get a vote if leadership doesn't want one.

The only way around the Speaker in the House is a discharge petition. If 218 members, a majority of the House, sign one, they can force a bill to the floor. It's rarely used, and it's especially rare for members of the majority party to sign one against their own leaders.

You've seen it work in this outlet's own reporting. The House vote to extend the health insurance tax credits in January (Part 1's "expiration dates") occurred only after a bipartisan discharge petition forced it. In 2025, members of both parties used petitions to force votes their leaders had blocked, including on the release of the Epstein files and on federal workers' bargaining rights, and Rep. Anna Paulina Luna, a Florida Republican, threatened to file one on a congressional stock-trading ban.

> *Editorial:* Think about what that means. A majority of the people's representatives have to sign a petition, against their own leaders, just to get a vote on something a majority already supports. That's not a two-party system. That's a two-leader system.

### Lock 10: The Incumbent

Finally, the people already in office are protected by the system they run.

In 2024, according to Ballotpedia, 97% of congressional incumbents who ran for reelection won. According to OpenSecrets data cited by Reason, House incumbents have won reelection more than 90% of the time over the past decade, and their reelection rate hasn't dropped below 85% in at least 60 years. And Gallup found only 32% of Americans trust Congress.

So Americans distrust Congress as a whole, and send back the same members almost every time. Safe districts (Lock 4), party-run primaries (Lock 3), the money (Lock 7), name recognition, and the built-in perks of office all point the same way. Even this year's wave, which flipped both chambers, left most incumbents in both parties in their seats. *(Update with the 2026 incumbent reelection rate when available.)*

### Voter Apathy Today

So how much of today's low turnout is apathy?

Some of it, surely. But look at what a voter is up against. In most districts, the general election is decided before it happens, and the money that floods the races that aren't is often impossible to trace. The primary that does decide it is closed to many of them. The candidates they preferred were kept off the ballot or the debate stage. And the people they elect answer to party leaders who control their committee seats.

> *Editorial:* That's not apathy. That's a rational response to a rigged game. When people believe their vote can't change anything, many stop casting it, and the parties are fine with that. A smaller, more partisan electorate is easier to manage. In Part 3, the machine bought votes. Today it doesn't have to. It just makes sure most votes don't matter.

### Removing the Locks

None of these locks is permanent. Every one of them is a law or a rule written by people, and laws and rules can be changed. Here's each lock in plain English: the problem, the fix, and what we should honestly expect if we make it.

**Lock 1: The ballot.**

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The problem:* Republican and Democratic nominees get on the ballot automatically. Everyone else has to earn it. In Texas, an independent candidate for governor needed 81,030 signatures, gathered within a few months from voters who didn't vote in either party's primary, before they could even start campaigning.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The fix:* One set of rules for every candidate. The same number of signatures, the same deadline, the same fee, whether you're a Republican, a Democrat, a Libertarian, or nobody's candidate. And let any registered voter sign any petition.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *What to expect:* More names on the ballot, and independent and third-party candidates spending their money on campaigning instead of on petition drives. The worry is a crowded ballot where someone wins with 35% of the vote. That's exactly the problem ranked-choice voting solves, below.

**Lock 2: The sore loser law.**

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The problem:* Lose a primary that a small, partisan slice of voters decided, and you're barred from asking everyone else for their vote in November.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The fix:* Repeal sore loser laws, including Texas Election Code Section 162.015. A party can refuse to nominate a candidate. It shouldn't be able to stop the voters from choosing them anyway.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *What to expect:* Most primary losers will still go home. Running in November without a party is hard and expensive. But a candidate with a real following, like Joe Lieberman in 2006 or Lisa Murkowski in 2010, gets to make the case to all the voters. Connecticut and New York have no sore loser laws, and their elections haven't descended into chaos. Primaries would matter a little less, and candidates would have to keep the whole electorate in mind, not just their party's base.

**Lock 3: The primary that decides everything.**

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The problem:* In most districts, the November election is a formality. The real decision is the majority party's primary, where about 7% of eligible voters picked most of the House in 2024, and in many states, independents can't vote at all.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The fix:* Open the primary to every voter and every candidate, and pair it with ranked-choice voting in November. (See "Explained: Primaries and Ranked Choice Voting" below.)

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *What to expect:* Candidates in safe districts would have to win over independents and voters from the other party, not just the most partisan voters in their own party. Honestly, the research on whether open primaries produce more moderate politicians is mixed. What they reliably do is give every voter a say in the election that actually decides who represents them. And the details matter: as you'll see below, one version of the open primary nearly wiped third parties off the November ballot.

**Lock 4: The map.**

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The problem:* Politicians draw their own district lines, so in most districts the voters don't pick their representative. The mapmakers already did.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The fix:* Independent redistricting commissions that neither party controls, with rules against drawing lines to help a party or an incumbent, and no override when the party in power doesn't like the result.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *What to expect:* More competitive districts, though not as many as people hope, because Americans increasingly live near people who vote like them. California is the best test. A 2018 study by the Public Policy Institute of California found the maps drawn by the state's citizens' commission were fair to both parties and more competitive than about 72% of other states' congressional maps. The catch is that a commission only works if the politicians can't take the job back. California's Democrats did exactly that in 2025 with Proposition 50, as described above. A real fix has to be written so neither party can switch it off when it's losing.

**Lock 5: The referees.**

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The problem:* The agencies that referee elections, from the FEC to the debate commission, have seats for Republicans and Democrats and no one else. And the 15% debate threshold is nearly impossible to reach without the attention that only the debates provide.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The fix:* Seats on the FEC and on debate commissions for people who belong to neither party. A debate threshold based on something a candidate can control, such as being on enough state ballots to win 270 electoral votes, or a lower polling bar.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *What to expect:* A third voice on the debate stage in some years, and referees that can't simply deadlock along party lines. The risk is a crowded stage. A ballot-access test keeps out candidates who aren't seriously running while giving voters a look at those who are. Ross Perot was on the stage in 1992, and the country survived just fine.

**Lock 6: Congress itself.**

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The problem:* Committee seats, where most of the real work in Congress happens, are handed out through the two-party caucuses. An independent who won't join one gets shut out.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The fix:* Give independents committee seats by House and Senate rule, in proportion to their numbers, without requiring them to caucus with either party.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *What to expect:* "Independent" would stop being a costume. Today, an independent who wants to get anything done has to join one team. With committee seats of their own, independents could vote on each bill on its merits, and the voters who sent them there would get what they voted for.

**Lock 7: The money.**

This lock is big enough to get its own chapter. Part 7, The Megaphone, covers the problem and the fixes, from real-time donor disclosure to labels on AI ads.

**Lock 8: The winner-take-all Electoral College.**

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The problem:* In 48 states, whoever wins the state's popular vote, even by a single vote, gets all of its electors. That's how Ross Perot won nearly 20 million votes and zero electoral votes.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The fix:* States can change this on their own. No constitutional amendment is needed. They can award electors by congressional district, like Maine and Nebraska, or in proportion to the statewide vote.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *What to expect:* Presidential candidates would campaign in more places, because a single district could be worth winning even in a state the candidate is going to lose. Maine and Nebraska show it working: Barack Obama won an electoral vote in Nebraska's 2nd District in 2008, Donald Trump won one in Maine's 2nd District in 2016, and both happened again in 2020. Two cautions, though. First, the district method uses congressional district lines, so it only works fairly if the maps are fair (Lock 4). Second, if only one party's states switch, it hands an advantage to the other party, which is why the change works best when states make it together. A proportional system would do the most for third parties: a candidate with nearly 1 in 5 votes would finally win some electors.

**Lock 9: Who controls the floor.**

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The problem:* Party leaders decide which bills get a vote. A bill can have majority support and still never reach the floor.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The fix:* Change the House and Senate rules so any bill backed by a majority of members gets a vote within a set time, or lower the number of signatures a discharge petition needs.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *What to expect:* Fewer bills buried by leadership, and more votes where members have to go on the record. Party leaders would lose their quiet veto. The cost is more floor time and some uncomfortable votes for both parties, which is exactly the point.

**Lock 10: The incumbent.**

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The problem:* Americans don't trust Congress, but they reelect nearly everyone in it. Safe maps, party primaries, money, and name recognition protect the people already in office.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The fix:* Mostly, the other nine. Fair maps, open primaries, ranked-choice voting, fair ballot access, and transparent money make incumbents compete. Some voters also want term limits for Congress, which would take a constitutional amendment.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *What to expect:* More competitive races and more turnover, without throwing out good members just because their time is up. Term limits are worth debating honestly. Supporters say they break up careers in Congress and bring in fresh people. Critics point to state legislatures with term limits, arguing that power shifted to lobbyists and staff, who never face term limits. Either way, the first nine fixes do more to make incumbents earn their seats.

### Explained: Primaries and Ranked Choice Voting

These two reforms come up together, and they're the ones people understand least. Here's how they work, in plain English.

**The four kinds of primaries.** A primary is the election before the election, where candidates compete to get on the November ballot. States run them in different ways:

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; **Closed:** Only voters registered with a party can vote in that party's primary. Independents are left out.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; **Semi-closed:** Party members vote in their own party's primary, and independents can pick one party's primary to vote in.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; **Open:** Any voter can choose either party's primary, but only one.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; **Top-two or top-four:** Every candidate from every party runs on a single primary ballot, every voter gets that same ballot, and the top two (or top four) finishers go on to November, regardless of party.

According to Ballotpedia, 14 states use open primaries for state and congressional races, 13 use closed ones, and others use mixed systems or let the parties decide. Texas has what's called an open primary: you don't register with a party, but when you vote in one party's primary, you're tied to that party for the rest of the year. That's why a Texan who votes in a primary can't sign an independent candidate's petition (Lock 1), or run as an independent for an office that was on that primary ballot (Lock 2).

**The trap in "top-two."** California and Washington put every candidate on one primary ballot and send the top two to November. It sounds open, and it is open to voters. But when California's Public Policy Institute studied the first top-two elections in 2012, it found the system "resulted in the near elimination of minor party candidates in the fall election." In many districts, November became a choice between two Democrats or two Republicans, with the Libertarian and Green candidates long gone. That's why Alaska chose a different design: send the top *four* to November, and use ranked choice voting to pick the winner.

**How ranked choice voting works.** Instead of picking one candidate, you rank them: first choice, second choice, third choice. You can rank as many or as few as you want. Then:

1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Count everyone's first choice. If one candidate has more than half, they win. Done.

2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; If no one has more than half, the candidate in last place is eliminated.

3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Every ballot that had that candidate first now counts for its second choice.

4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Count again, and repeat until someone has more than half.

**An example.** Say 100 Texans vote in a race with three candidates: a Republican, a Democrat, and a Libertarian. Here are the first choices:

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Republican: 45

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Democrat: 40

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Libertarian: 15

No one has more than half (51). So the Libertarian, in last place, is eliminated, and those 15 ballots go to each voter's second choice. Say 10 of them ranked the Republican second and 5 ranked the Democrat second. The new count:

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Republican: 45 + 10 = 55

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Democrat: 40 + 5 = 45

The Republican wins with a majority.

Notice what happened. The 15 Libertarian voters got to vote for the candidate they actually believed in, and their votes still counted in the final round. Nobody "wasted" a vote. Nobody "spoiled" anything. Under today's rules, those voters would have had to choose between voting their conscience and voting against the candidate they feared most. That's the "wasted vote" fear from Part 1, and ranked-choice voting takes it away.

Texans already accept the idea behind it. When no one wins a majority in a Texas primary, we hold a runoff weeks later between the top two, usually with far fewer voters. Ranked-choice voting is sometimes called an "instant runoff" because it does the same thing on the same ballot, on the same day, for everyone who voted.

**Where it's been used.** Maine has used ranked choice voting for federal races and primaries since 2018. That first year, it decided a congressional race for the first time: Republican Rep. Bruce Poliquin led on the first count, but Democrat Jared Golden surpassed 50% after second-choice votes were counted. Poliquin sued to stop the count and lost. Alaska adopted top-four primaries and ranked-choice voting in 2020. In a 2022 special election, Democrat Mary Peltola won the state's House seat after two Republicans, Sarah Palin and Nick Begich, split the Republican vote. Alaska voters kept the system in 2024 by 743 votes out of more than 320,000, and early returns this week showed them keeping it again. New York City uses it for its primaries.

**The case against, fairly stated.** Ranked-choice voting has serious critics, and they make real points:

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *It's more complicated.* Ranking candidates takes more thought than marking one, and counting takes longer, so results can take days.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *Some ballots drop out.* If you rank only candidates who get eliminated, your ballot doesn't count in the final round. Critics call these "exhausted" ballots. In San Francisco's 2011 mayor's race, about 27% of ballots were exhausted before the last round, according to an analysis in *City Journal*. That means the "majority" a winner gets may be a majority of the ballots still in play, not of everyone who voted.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The first-place candidate can lose,* as Bruce Poliquin did in Maine, which feels wrong to many voters.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *The research on its benefits is mixed.* Studies haven't consistently shown that it raises turnout or produces more moderate winners.

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; *It's being banned.* As of March 2026, 19 states had passed laws banning ranked-choice voting, according to Ballotpedia, mostly in Republican-led states, following the results in Alaska and Maine. In Texas, the Secretary of State in 2001 and Attorney General Greg Abbott in 2003 concluded that state law doesn't allow it. That's why Austin couldn't use it after nearly 60% of its voters approved it in 2021. In 2025, the Texas Senate passed a bill to make that ban explicit law.

> *Editorial:* Every one of those criticisms is fair, and none of them outweighs what ranked-choice voting does: it lets you vote for who you actually want, without helping the candidate you fear most. Look at who's banning it. After Alaska's 2022 special election, Republicans turned against it. Democrats have their own history of fighting reforms that threaten their seats, as the 2024 ballot measures in Lock 3 showed. When both parties fight a reform this hard, ask yourself who it threatens. The answer is them.

**The bottom line.** Each lock is a rule, and each rule has a fix. None of the fixes guarantees that independents or third parties will win. They guarantee something more important: that the voters, not the two parties, decide.

**A warning for Texans.** In Part 4, I said voters in initiative states can use the citizen initiative to go around legislatures that won’t reform themselves, and I noted that Texas isn’t one of those states. Here’s what that means. According to Ballotpedia, “Citizens of Texas do not have the power to initiate statewide ballot measures." Constitutional amendments reach the Texas ballot only if two-thirds of both chambers of the Legislature put them there, at least 100 House members and 21 senators. That means every one of these fixes has to pass through the same two parties it would weaken. The first reform Texans should demand is the right to put reforms on the ballot themselves. And Texas lawmakers have shown they can loosen the party's grip when they want to: in 2017, the Legislature passed, and Gov. Greg Abbott signed, House Bill 25, which ended straight-ticket voting starting in 2020. In the 2016 general election, straight-ticket voting had accounted for nearly 64% of the votes in the state's 10 largest counties. Supporters said ending it would lead voters to make more informed, race-by-race choices; opponents said it would hurt minority voters. Either way, it shows a party-run legislature can pass a reform that weakens party-line voting.

### Discussion Questions for the Comments

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Should a voter who cast a ballot in a party primary be barred from signing an independent candidate's petition?

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Is a "bipartisan" commission fair if it has no seat for anyone outside the two parties?

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Both parties redrew maps in 2025 and said the other side started it. Who should draw the lines?

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Joe Lieberman and Lisa Murkowski both lost their primaries and won in November. Should Texans be allowed to do what voters in Connecticut and Alaska did?

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Is money speech? If it is, should we at least know who's paying for it?

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Ross Perot won nearly 1 in 5 votes in 1992 and zero electoral votes. Is that fair?

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Should Texans have the right to put laws on the ballot themselves, the way voters in Oregon and South Dakota can?

•&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Would you rather have a runoff weeks later, or rank your choices on the same ballot? Why?

If you had to pick just one lock to break first, which one would it be?

